  {
   "id": "m4-27",
   "name": "Use the laws of indices and solve simple exponential equations"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Laws of indices: aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ; aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ; (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ; a⁰ = 1; a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ; a^(1/n) is the nth root.\nAn exponential equation has the unknown in the index, such as 2ˣ = 32.\nMethod: write both sides as powers of the same base, then the indices are equal.\n• 2ˣ = 32 = 2⁵, so x = 5.\n• 3ˣ⁺¹ = 81 = 3⁴, so x + 1 = 4 and x = 3.\n• 4ˣ = 8: 4 = 2², 8 = 2³, so 2²ˣ = 2³ and x = 3/2.\n• 9ˣ = 1/27: 3²ˣ = 3⁻³, so x = −3/2.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "learn": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "sections": [
   {
    "heading": "Your goal and connections",
    "text": "By the end, you should be able to use the laws of indices and solve exponential equations by writing both sides as powers of the same base.\nBuilds on: Form Three laws of indices (zero, negative and fractional indices).\nLeads to: Lesson {28} (logarithms), which solves exponential equations when the bases cannot be matched."
   },
   {
    "heading": "The laws, revised",
    "text": "For any base a:\n• aᵐ × aⁿ = a{sup('m+n')}  • aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = a{sup('m−n')}  • (aᵐ)ⁿ = a{sup('mn')}\n• a{sup(0)} = {1}  • a{sup(-1)} = {1}/a, a{sup(-2)} = {1}/a²  • a{sup('1/2')} = √a\nExample: {2}{sup(5)} × {2}{sup(3)} ÷ {2}{sup(4)} = {2}{sup(4)} = {16}.\nThese work only for the same base: {2}{sup(3)} × {3}{sup(2)} cannot be combined into one power."
   },
   {
    "heading": "Exponential equations",
    "text": "When x is in the index, make the bases match, then the indices must be equal.\n• {3}{sup('x+1')} = {81}: {81} = {3}{sup(4)}, so x + {1} = {4}, x = {3}.\n• {4}ˣ = {8}: {4} = {2}{sup(2)} and {8} = {2}{sup(3)}, so {2}{sup('2x')} = {2}{sup(3)}, {2}x = {3}, x = {frac(3, 2)}.\n• {9}ˣ = {1}/{27}: {3}{sup('2x')} = {3}{sup(-3)}, so x = {frac(-3, 2)}.\nCheck by putting the answer back: {4}{sup('3/2')} = (√{4})³ = {8}."
   }
  ],
  "key_words": [
   {
    "word": "index (power)",
    "meaning": "the small raised number: the {3} in {2}{sup(3)}"
   },
   {
    "word": "base",
    "meaning": "the number being raised to the power"
   },
   {
    "word": "exponential equation",
    "meaning": "an equation with the unknown in the index"
   }
  ],
  "examples": [
   "{5}ˣ = {125} → x = {3}",
   "{8}ˣ = {4} → {2}{sup('3x')} = {2}{sup(2)} → x = {frac(2, 3)}",
   "{2}ˣ = {frac(1, 8)} → x = {-3}"
  ],
  "mistake": {
   "wrong": "{4}ˣ = {8} → x = {2}",
   "right": "{4}ˣ = {8} → x = {frac(3, 2)}",
   "why": "{4}{sup(2)} = {16}, not {8}. Write both as powers of {2}: {2}{sup('2x')} = {2}{sup(3)}."
  }
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "p": 2,
   "q": 3
  },
  "problem": "Solve {2 ^ p}ˣ = {2 ^ q}.",
  "steps": [
   "Write both sides as powers of {2}: {2 ^ p} = {2}{sup(p)} and {2 ^ q} = {2}{sup(q)}.",
   "So ({2}{sup(p)})ˣ = {2}{sup(q)}, that is {2}{sup('2x')} = {2}{sup(q)}.",
   "The indices are equal: {p}x = {q}."
  ],
  "answer": "x = {frac(q, p)}",
  "checks": [
   "2 ^ p == 4",
   "2 ^ q == 8"
  ]
 },
